<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tswana people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Batswana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tswana are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> who speak Setswana and form the majority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>'s population, with large communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s North West province. They are organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahurutshe">Bahurutshe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a>, Bangwato and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a>, which grew from shared lineages by successive splits. Their large towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> wealth and kgotla assemblies shaped the history of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tswana-people.jpg" alt="Tswana people" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seven_Years_in_South_Africa,_page_454,_Bella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before colonial rule, Tswana-speaking communities were organised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, merafe, often built round clan lineages and governed by dikgosi, chiefs, with advisory councils. In the early nineteenth century the Difaqane, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>, a period of warfare and displacement from 1815 to 1840, raided or moved many Tswana settlements; it contributed to stronger centralised states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a> and Bangwato.</p><p>In the 1840s and 1850s trade with merchants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> rebuild. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a>, Bangwato and Batawana worked together to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade and used the proceeds to buy horses and guns, which let them dominate what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> by about 1880, subjugating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">Bushmen</a>, Bakalanga, Bakgalagadi and Batswapong.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> settled on the borders of the Tswana lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>. In 1852 a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sechele-i">Sechele I</a> resisted them at the Battle of Dimawe, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> commando retreated; after about eight years of tension a peace agreement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potchefstroom">Potchefstroom</a> in 1860 settled the modern border.</p><p>In 1884 the Batawana under Kgosi Moremi, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wayeyi">Wayeyi</a> allies, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> raiders at Khutiyabasadi. In 1893 and 1894 some 700 Tswana allies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> in the First Matabele War. European missionaries had established stations from the mid-nineteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread as several dikgosi adopted it or allowed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kgotla, a public assembly where disputes are settled and decisions debated, was the central institution of governance, though it largely excluded women from formal debate. Descent was patrilineal. Young people were incorporated into age-regiments called mephato; boys were circumcised and, on completing their training, were brought to the kgotla, given a regimental name and presented with weapons. Girls passed through the initiation called bojale. Traditional belief centred on Modimo, a supreme spiritual force, and Badimo, the ancestral spirits. Marriage was formalised by bogadi, bridewealth usually paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> porridge, bogobe, is the staple food.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, formerly the British protectorate of Bechuanaland, is named for the Tswana, and ethnic Tswana made up about 85 per cent of its population in 2011. The largest number of Tswana live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, where Setswana is one of eleven official languages. From 1948 to 1994 the apartheid government defined South African Tswana as citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a>, one of ten bantustans. It was granted nominal independence on 6 December 1977 and reincorporated into South Africa on 27 April 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tswana people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bahurutshe trace their descent from the founding <b>Tswana</b> lineage, from which the Bakwena and other groups later branched.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahurutshe-chiefdom">Bahurutshe Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Fearing Boer expansion, the <b>Tswana</b> chiefs accepted British protection, and in 1885 Kweneng became part of the Bechuanaland Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/bakwena-chiefdom">Bakwena Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Makaba II in the late 18th and early 19th centuries they became one of the strongest <b>Tswana</b> states, until Makaba was killed in 1824 during the wars of that era.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangwaketse-chiefdom">Bangwaketse Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Barolong are among the oldest <b>Tswana</b> groups, settled in the southern Kalahari margins and the Molopo valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/barolong-chiefdom">Barolong Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tswana</b> rulers such as Khama III of the Bangwato Kingdom welcomed protection against the Boers and the Ndebele.</blockquote><a href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>Much land was allocated to white farmers, while reserves were set aside for <b>Tswana</b> chiefs such as Montshiwa.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-bechuanaland">British Bechuanaland</a></li></ul></section>
